.TH HUBPORTS 8
.SH NAME
hubports \- ask a USB hub what it sees on each of its ports
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B usb/hubports
.I epN.M
[
.I nports
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Hubports
sends a hub-class
.B GET_STATUS
to a USB hub for each of its ports and prints the reply decoded:
whether a device is attached, at what speed, whether the port is enabled
or powered, and the port's change bits.
.I EpN.M
is the hub's control endpoint as named in
.BR /dev/usb ,
for instance
.B ep2.0
for the Raspberry Pi 3B's onboard LAN9514.
.PP
The port count comes from the hub descriptor when
.I nports
is not given, so a wrong guess cannot silently shorten the survey \- a
survey that stops early looks exactly like a hub with fewer ports.
.PP
The output names the speed in full, and says which speeds need a split
transaction:
.IP
.EX
hubports: ep2.0: hub descriptor says 5 ports
hubports: ep2.0 port 1: status 0103 change 0000 present enabled high-speed (no split)
hubports: ep2.0 port 2: status 0101 change 0001 present DISABLED FULL-SPEED (needs split)
hubports: ep2.0 port 3: status 0000 change 0000 empty
.EE
.PP
Neither speed bit set means full speed; there is no "full" bit.
.SH "WHY IT EXISTS"
An investigation into full-speed devices failing behind the Pi 3B's
onboard hub ran twelve times on one physical port and recorded twelve
failures, while every port that worked had only ever carried a
high-speed device. Nothing in that population distinguishes "the
split-transaction path is broken" from "that port is broken", because
the port was never varied.
.PP
A hub's own
.B GET_STATUS
separates them and needs no device moved: the request goes to the
.I hub
at high speed, with no split transaction anywhere in it, so it
exercises a path the board is known to serve. A port reporting a
full-speed device present is a port doing its job, which moves the fault
downstream into the split path; a port reporting nothing, with a device
plugged into it, is the port.
.PP
It is read-only on purpose.
.B GET_STATUS
changes no state, so it is safe to run against a live hub on a board
whose only recovery is a power cycle.
.SH "WHAT IS REAL AND WHAT IS NOT"
.B "The tool works and has been run; its decoding has not been checked"
.B "against a hub that reports a fault."
Every reading taken so far is of healthy high-speed ports or of QEMU's
hub, which reports all of its ports present and unreset before
enumeration. The
.B "the hub would not answer"
and
.B OVERCURRENT
arms have never fired.
.PP
.B "It cannot run beside a live"
.IR usbd (8)
.BR "on this kernel."
.I Usbd
keeps a data fd open for every device it attaches, so the hub's control
endpoint is
.B Einuse
and
.I hubports
fails with
.BR "device or object already in use" .
Killing
.I usbd
does not help: the endpoints stay
.B busy
with no
.B Usbd
process in
.IR ps ,
which is a separate open issue. So this is a tool for a boot where
.I usbd
is not started.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/usb/hubports.b
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR usb (3)
.SH BUGS
The 16-bit status and change words are printed as the hub returns them,
with no attempt to reconcile USB 2.0 and USB 3 port bit assignments; on
a USB 3 hub the speed decode is meaningless.
